1
00:00:07,799 --> 00:00:10,419
There were four of them, and they
were very good.

2
00:00:10,989 --> 00:00:16,675
They had been soldiers once — two of them in the
same special operations unit, two in

3
00:00:16,695 --> 00:00:22,382
the British Army — and then they had become the
kind of men that large companies hire

4
00:00:22,402 --> 00:00:28,357
when they need something done that nobody will ever
write down. They worked for a private

5
00:00:28,377 --> 00:00:34,131
security firm in Virginia that had been retained,
through three layers of consultants,

6
00:00:34,151 --> 00:00:39,838
by the competitive intelligence department of
Halvorsen Pike. They had been told that

7
00:00:39,858 --> 00:00:45,746
the job was simple: enter a research facility in
Wilmington with the help of an insider,

8
00:00:45,766 --> 00:00:51,990
copy the contents of a single air-gapped server,
photograph anything unusual in the basement,

9
00:00:52,010 --> 00:00:52,661
and leave.

10
00:00:53,231 --> 00:00:58,377
They had been told it would take twelve minutes. They
had been told nobody would be hurt.

11
00:00:58,948 --> 00:01:03,642
Both of these things were believed, sincerely,
by the people who had told them.

12
00:01:05,712 --> 00:01:11,087
Howard Lessing let them in at twelve fifty-two,
through the loading dock door, with his

13
00:01:11,107 --> 00:01:11,708
own badge.

14
00:01:12,278 --> 00:01:17,685
He had spent three weeks telling himself why. He
had told himself that the Bureau was

15
00:01:17,705 --> 00:01:23,112
too slow, that the Bureau had been captured, that
the Bureau would chase shipments of

16
00:01:23,132 --> 00:01:28,540
liquid nitrogen until everybody was dead. He had
told himself that four professionals

17
00:01:28,560 --> 00:01:34,222
with thermal cameras and ground-penetrating equipment
would see what his phone could not.

18
00:01:34,242 --> 00:01:39,714
That they would come out with photographs of
whatever was under the basement, and that

19
00:01:39,734 --> 00:01:45,269
he would take those photographs to Owen Carrick, and
to Hannah Price, and to the world.

20
00:01:45,289 --> 00:01:48,844
That he would finally, finally be the
one who was right.

21
00:01:49,415 --> 00:01:55,149
He had not told himself what Halvorsen Pike was
paying him. He did not need to. It was

22
00:01:55,169 --> 00:02:01,037
a great deal of money, and it was already in an
account in Cyprus, and he was trying not

23
00:02:01,057 --> 00:02:02,242
to think about it.

24
00:02:04,311 --> 00:02:06,528
They went down to the basement first.

25
00:02:07,099 --> 00:02:13,198
It was dark, and cold, and it smelled of refrigerant
and old concrete. The four men moved

26
00:02:13,218 --> 00:02:19,179
through it the way they had been trained to move
through houses in other countries — in

27
00:02:19,199 --> 00:02:25,092
a line, weapons low, each covering a sector. Their
thermal cameras swept the freezers,

28
00:02:25,112 --> 00:02:30,523
the new vault, the bare concrete corner where a
grey machine had once breathed.

29
00:02:31,213 --> 00:02:36,450
Contact. There's heat under the floor. A lot of
heat. Big volume. Thirty, forty metres

30
00:02:36,470 --> 00:02:36,756
down.

31
00:02:37,156 --> 00:02:39,686
That's not a basement. That's a building.

32
00:02:41,206 --> 00:02:46,480
Lessing, standing behind them by the freight
elevator, felt something in his chest go

33
00:02:46,500 --> 00:02:50,714
very light. He had been right. He had been
right. They could see it.

34
00:02:52,284 --> 00:02:55,861
And then, behind him, the freight
elevator panel lit up.

35
00:02:56,431 --> 00:03:02,005
He turned. He saw it happen — the thing he had tried
for three months to photograph, the

36
00:03:02,025 --> 00:03:07,599
thing he had begun to believe he had imagined. Below
the button for the basement, in the

37
00:03:07,619 --> 00:03:13,129
smooth steel plate with the four screws, a third
button was glowing. Black, with a thin

38
00:03:13,149 --> 00:03:19,040
crimson ring. And the floor indicator above the doors,
which had only ever shown two numbers,

39
00:03:19,060 --> 00:03:22,599
was showing a third one, and the
third one was climbing.

40
00:03:23,169 --> 00:03:24,675
Something was coming up.

41
00:03:26,566 --> 00:03:29,042
Oh, God. Oh, God, he's here.

42
00:03:30,361 --> 00:03:32,412
What? Who's here? Doc — who's here?

43
00:03:32,932 --> 00:03:38,613
Lessing did not answer. He was backing away from the
elevator. He was making a sound —

44
00:03:38,633 --> 00:03:44,314
a thin, high sound, like a kettle. One of the
soldiers, a young man from Tennessee who

45
00:03:44,334 --> 00:03:50,080
had been awake for thirty hours and had been told
nobody would be here, swung his rifle

46
00:03:50,100 --> 00:03:55,914
toward the noise, and toward the glowing panel, and
toward the old man who was suddenly,

47
00:03:55,934 --> 00:03:57,504
inexplicably, screaming.

48
00:03:59,575 --> 00:04:05,168
He fired three times. He would say afterwards — to
the one person he ever told — that

49
00:04:05,188 --> 00:04:11,309
he had not meant to. That the old man had moved. That
he had thought the old man was reaching

50
00:04:11,329 --> 00:04:12,234
for something.

51
00:04:12,802 --> 00:04:18,246
Howard Lessing fell against the freezers, and slid
down them, and sat on the cold floor

52
00:04:18,266 --> 00:04:23,709
of the basement where he had spent thirty-four years
of his working life, with his hand

53
00:04:23,729 --> 00:04:25,027
pressed to his chest.

54
00:04:25,598 --> 00:04:30,245
He was looking at the elevator. At the black
button. At the number climbing.

55
00:04:30,814 --> 00:04:36,497
He had been right. He had wanted, more than anything
in his life, to be the one who was

56
00:04:36,517 --> 00:04:42,658
right, and he was. It was the last thing he knew, and —
the Cold-Read would tell the Director,

57
00:04:42,678 --> 00:04:48,623
thirty seconds later, reading what was left in the old
man's face — he died, of all things,

58
00:04:48,643 --> 00:04:49,279
satisfied.

59
00:04:51,848 --> 00:04:53,656
The elevator doors opened.

60
00:04:54,226 --> 00:04:59,684
He had been in Arklay. He had been working late, as
he did every Sunday, and Hale had

61
00:04:59,704 --> 00:05:05,161
told him eleven seconds ago that the loading dock
door had opened on Doctor Lessing's

62
00:05:05,181 --> 00:05:10,703
badge with four men behind him. He had been in the
elevator when the shots were fired.

63
00:05:10,723 --> 00:05:13,022
He had heard them through the shaft.

64
00:05:13,593 --> 00:05:19,494
He stepped out. He saw Howard Lessing on the floor.
He saw four men with rifles. And he

65
00:05:19,514 --> 00:05:25,279
felt — for the first time in either of his lives —
two completely different things at

66
00:05:25,299 --> 00:05:26,981
exactly the same instant.

67
00:05:28,050 --> 00:05:33,721
The Wesker half felt fury. Cold, exact,
professional fury, the fury of a chess player

68
00:05:33,741 --> 00:05:39,948
whose opponent has knocked the board over. Because this
could not be hidden. A dead employee.

69
00:05:39,968 --> 00:05:45,907
Four armed men. Gunshots. Nothing about this could
ever be made to disappear. Three years

70
00:05:45,927 --> 00:05:51,732
of silence, of deniability, of controlled burns and
clean rooms and sculptures in glass

71
00:05:51,752 --> 00:05:57,490
cases — ended, by a frightened boy from Tennessee
who had been awake for thirty hours.

72
00:05:58,060 --> 00:06:00,033
And the Adolf half felt joy.

73
00:06:01,721 --> 00:06:02,981
At last.

74
00:06:04,002 --> 00:06:09,643
He used the Thought Acceleration. He had not planned
to. It simply happened, the way a

75
00:06:09,663 --> 00:06:12,474
hand closes when something is
thrown at it.

76
00:06:13,045 --> 00:06:18,943
The world slowed to a sixtieth of its speed. The
muzzle flash of the first rifle blossomed

77
00:06:18,963 --> 00:06:24,796
in the dark like a slow yellow flower. He could see
the bullet leave the barrel. He could

78
00:06:24,816 --> 00:06:30,649
see the young man's face behind it — terror, and
exhaustion, and, beneath both, a flicker

79
00:06:30,669 --> 00:06:34,529
of confusion that he was already too
late to finish having.

80
00:06:35,098 --> 00:06:40,951
He stepped to the left. It took, in subjective time,
about two seconds. In the time of

81
00:06:40,971 --> 00:06:43,819
the room, it did not take any time at all.

82
00:06:44,788 --> 00:06:48,949
The first man died without understanding
that anyone had moved.

83
00:06:49,519 --> 00:06:55,339
The second was thrown. He hit the door of a freezer
from the nineteen-nineties hard enough

84
00:06:55,359 --> 00:06:57,350
to fold it, and did not get up.

85
00:06:57,920 --> 00:07:03,794
The third — the boy from Tennessee — got off one more
shot, which went into the ceiling,

86
00:07:03,814 --> 00:07:09,486
before a gloved hand closed on his rifle and bent
the barrel ninety degrees, and then

87
00:07:09,506 --> 00:07:10,893
closed on his throat.

88
00:07:12,963 --> 00:07:14,228
The fourth ran.

89
00:07:14,798 --> 00:07:20,348
He was the oldest of them, and the most experienced,
and he had seen enough in the dark

90
00:07:20,368 --> 00:07:26,046
of the basement to understand, instantly and
completely, that nothing he had been trained

91
00:07:26,066 --> 00:07:31,488
for was going to help him. He ran up the ramp, and
out through the loading dock door,

92
00:07:31,508 --> 00:07:36,418
and across the parking lot, toward the van,
toward the road, toward anything.

93
00:07:36,987 --> 00:07:43,346
It was twelve fifty-six. The parking lot was empty.
The access road beyond it was not.

94
00:07:45,415 --> 00:07:51,569
A woman named Keisha Bell was driving north on the
access road at that moment, in a grey

95
00:07:51,589 --> 00:07:57,953
Toyota, taking a rideshare passenger home from a bar
in Trolley Square. She had a dashboard

96
00:07:57,973 --> 00:08:04,477
camera, because she drove for a living and had been
rear-ended twice. It recorded everything,

97
00:08:04,497 --> 00:08:07,283
all the time, at thirty frames a second.

98
00:08:07,853 --> 00:08:13,604
It recorded a man in dark clothing sprinting across
the Umbrella parking lot, from left

99
00:08:13,624 --> 00:08:15,860
to right, under the sodium lights.

100
00:08:16,430 --> 00:08:18,630
And then it recorded something else.

101
00:08:20,699 --> 00:08:26,235
Frame one thousand one hundred and ten: the man,
running, forty metres from the loading

102
00:08:26,255 --> 00:08:31,662
dock door. Frame one thousand one hundred and nine:
a shape at the loading dock door.

103
00:08:31,682 --> 00:08:37,217
Frame one thousand one hundred and twelve: a dark
smear, long and horizontal, stretched

104
00:08:37,237 --> 00:08:42,773
across a third of the parking lot, like a shadow
that had been torn. Frame one thousand

105
00:08:42,793 --> 00:08:48,392
one hundred and thirteen: the smear, longer. Frame
one thousand one hundred and fourteen

106
00:08:48,412 --> 00:08:48,455
—

107
00:08:50,025 --> 00:08:55,600
Frame one thousand one hundred and fourteen showed a
tall figure in a long black coat,

108
00:08:55,620 --> 00:09:01,259
standing perfectly still beside the running man,
with one hand on the back of his neck.

109
00:09:01,831 --> 00:09:07,329
In frame one thousand one hundred and fifteen, the
running man was on the ground. The

110
00:09:07,349 --> 00:09:10,835
figure in the coat was looking
directly at the camera.

111
00:09:13,404 --> 00:09:19,086
Keisha Bell did not see any of it. She was watching
the road. Her passenger was asleep.

112
00:09:19,106 --> 00:09:25,049
The car went on, north, toward the city, and neither
of them knew that they had just driven

113
00:09:25,069 --> 00:09:28,129
past the end of the world they
had grown up in.

114
00:09:30,698 --> 00:09:36,536
He stood in the parking lot for a long time, looking
at the tail lights of the grey Toyota

115
00:09:36,556 --> 00:09:37,773
disappearing north.

116
00:09:38,342 --> 00:09:44,967
He knew about dashcams. Tomás had taught him about
dashcams in the autumn of twenty twenty-six,

117
00:09:44,987 --> 00:09:49,864
in a break room, with a phone. He knew
exactly what that car had seen.

118
00:09:50,433 --> 00:09:56,275
There was no fixing it. He had already run through the
possibilities, in the Thought Acceleration,

119
00:09:56,295 --> 00:10:01,659
in the time it had taken the car to pass. He could
find the car. He could find the driver.

120
00:10:01,679 --> 00:10:06,862
He could make the driver and the passenger and the
footage disappear. And that would be

121
00:10:06,882 --> 00:10:12,545
five bodies instead of four, and two missing persons,
and a rideshare company asking questions,

122
00:10:12,565 --> 00:10:18,048
and a pattern — and a pattern was the one thing he had
spent three years never, ever leaving

123
00:10:18,068 --> 00:10:18,467
behind.

124
00:10:20,158 --> 00:10:23,293
Four. In forty seconds. Did you feel it?

125
00:10:23,813 --> 00:10:25,968
I felt a very expensive mistake.

126
00:10:26,488 --> 00:10:32,104
You felt what you are. For three years you have
hidden it behind contracts and laminated

127
00:10:32,124 --> 00:10:37,484
cards. Tonight you were what you are. I have been
waiting for that since the bunker.

128
00:10:38,004 --> 00:10:43,340
Tonight, a frightened boy killed the only man in
this company who could have sent me to

129
00:10:43,360 --> 00:10:48,572
prison, and I killed four men in front of a camera.
That isn't what I am. That's what

130
00:10:48,592 --> 00:10:50,358
I'm going to have to explain.

131
00:10:52,378 --> 00:10:58,104
He took out his phone. And Albert Wesker — who had
come into this world determined to

132
00:10:58,124 --> 00:11:04,189
be seen by no one — did the one thing that the Adolf
half of the mind had never, in either

133
00:11:04,209 --> 00:11:06,556
of their lives, expected him to do.

134
00:11:07,125 --> 00:11:08,845
He called the police himself.

135
00:11:10,914 --> 00:11:17,429
By two-forty in the morning there were seven police
vehicles in the parking lot, two ambulances,

136
00:11:17,449 --> 00:11:23,759
a coroner's van, and a Delaware State Police helicopter
circling overhead with a searchlight.

137
00:11:24,330 --> 00:11:29,626
The Director was waiting for them in the lobby of
the old building, in his black coat,

138
00:11:29,646 --> 00:11:34,880
with his sunglasses on. There was no blood on him.
There was not a hair out of place.

139
00:11:35,451 --> 00:11:41,594
He had told the dispatcher, calmly and in full
sentences, exactly what had happened. Armed

140
00:11:41,614 --> 00:11:47,690
intruders. A senior scientist shot dead. Four
intruders dead. He had not mentioned speed.

141
00:11:47,710 --> 00:11:53,717
He had not needed to. Four men with rifles, and one
unarmed chief executive who was fit,

142
00:11:53,737 --> 00:11:59,196
and trained, and very lucky, and had defended
himself and his staff in the dark.

143
00:11:59,765 --> 00:12:03,576
That was the story. It would be the
story for several days.

144
00:12:04,145 --> 00:12:10,022
Owen Carrick arrived at three-fifteen, in a coat
thrown over a T-shirt, having driven

145
00:12:10,042 --> 00:12:16,334
from Media at a hundred miles an hour after the call
from the Bureau's night desk — because

146
00:12:16,354 --> 00:12:22,577
the dead scientist was his confidential human source,
and the dead intruders were carrying

147
00:12:22,597 --> 00:12:25,629
rifles, and the Bureau was
taking the scene.

148
00:12:26,200 --> 00:12:31,926
He saw Lessing first. On the basement floor, against
the freezers, under a sheet. Somebody

149
00:12:31,946 --> 00:12:37,417
lifted the sheet for him. The old man's face was
peaceful. Carrick would remember that

150
00:12:37,437 --> 00:12:40,610
for the rest of his life, and
never understand it.

151
00:12:42,679 --> 00:12:47,832
Then he went up to the lobby, and found the Director
waiting for him, standing among the

152
00:12:47,852 --> 00:12:50,008
police as though he had invited them.

153
00:12:50,697 --> 00:12:51,697
Agent Carrick.

154
00:12:52,217 --> 00:12:58,043
He said it with great gentleness. He said it the way
you say something to a man at a funeral.

155
00:12:58,734 --> 00:13:00,655
I'm so sorry about your informant.

156
00:13:03,174 --> 00:13:08,707
Carrick did not say anything. He could not. He stood
in the lobby with the red and blue

157
00:13:08,727 --> 00:13:14,259
lights moving across both their faces, and he
understood — completely, and all at once,

158
00:13:14,279 --> 00:13:19,811
the way you understand that you have been falling
for a long time only when you hit the

159
00:13:19,831 --> 00:13:25,427
ground — that the man in front of him had known about
Howard Lessing from the very first

160
00:13:25,447 --> 00:13:25,682
day.

161
00:13:26,253 --> 00:13:31,760
Had known, and had let it happen. Had let him wear
the wire. Had fed him three months

162
00:13:31,780 --> 00:13:34,297
of liquid nitrogen and healthy monkeys.

163
00:13:34,866 --> 00:13:40,660
And was telling him so now, in front of a dozen
police officers, in a voice so kind that

164
00:13:40,680 --> 00:13:45,284
no one else in the room would ever hear it
as anything but condolence.

165
00:13:48,105 --> 00:13:51,584
Assessment. Deniability compromised.

166
00:13:57,044 --> 00:14:02,714
It was the first time the System had ever taken
anything away from him. He read that line

167
00:14:02,734 --> 00:14:08,723
for a long time, standing in the lobby, with the police
moving around him and the helicopter's

168
00:14:08,743 --> 00:14:10,832
searchlight sweeping the windows.

169
00:14:12,153 --> 00:14:14,980
Purchase confirmed. Immunity.

170
00:14:17,043 --> 00:14:22,246
He bought the immunity that night, for no reason he
could have explained, except that

171
00:14:22,266 --> 00:14:26,487
a man who is going to be on television
should be difficult to poison.

172
00:14:28,557 --> 00:14:34,194
Keisha Bell's passenger woke up on Monday
afternoon, and saw the news, and remembered

173
00:14:34,214 --> 00:14:40,250
that the driver had a dashcam, and asked her,
half-joking, whether she had caught anything.

174
00:14:40,820 --> 00:14:47,330
She had. She posted it on Monday night, with the
caption: wtf did my dashcam catch last

175
00:14:47,350 --> 00:14:53,785
night outside that umbrella place. By Tuesday
morning it had been watched nine million

176
00:14:53,805 --> 00:14:54,235
times.

177
00:14:54,806 --> 00:15:00,788
The internet called it the ghost video. And in a dark
office in Philadelphia, at ten past

178
00:15:00,808 --> 00:15:06,521
five on Tuesday morning, Owen Carrick sat with a
cold cup of coffee and watched frame

179
00:15:06,541 --> 00:15:10,906
one thousand one hundred and fourteen for
the two hundredth time.

180
00:15:11,475 --> 00:15:17,426
A figure in a long black coat. Standing perfectly
still. Forty metres from the door it

181
00:15:17,446 --> 00:15:23,326
had been standing at, four frames — a hundred and
thirty-three milliseconds — before.

182
00:15:23,897 --> 00:15:29,714
Forty metres in a hundred and thirty-three
milliseconds. He had done the arithmetic on

183
00:15:29,734 --> 00:15:35,551
a legal pad twenty-two times. It came to three
hundred metres a second. Just under the

184
00:15:35,571 --> 00:15:36,570
speed of sound.

185
00:15:38,639 --> 00:15:44,403
Owen Carrick had spent seventeen years building a
career on the principle that there is

186
00:15:44,423 --> 00:15:50,119
always a rational explanation. He had built it
carefully, one promotion at a time, and

187
00:15:50,139 --> 00:15:56,235
he had just been passed over for the job he wanted, and
his informant was dead on a basement

188
00:15:56,255 --> 00:16:02,151
floor, and he was looking at a frame of video that no
rational explanation could survive.

189
00:16:02,721 --> 00:16:08,978
He did not close the file. He never would. That
morning, at ten past five, in the dark,

190
00:16:08,998 --> 00:16:12,803
Special Agent Owen Carrick stopped
being a careerist.

191
00:16:13,373 --> 00:16:18,800
He became something else. Something the Bureau did
not have a word for, and would spend

192
00:16:18,820 --> 00:16:22,056
the next sixteen years trying and
failing to manage.

193
00:16:22,625 --> 00:16:24,321
He became a man who knew.
